WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.527
The NULL checkpoint reviews one of the most practical lessons in the module.

00:00:04.527 --> 00:00:10.107
NOT IN can behave unexpectedly when NULL appears in the returned set.

00:00:10.107 --> 00:00:14.702
NOT EXISTS tests whether a matching row exists and avoids that classic issue.

00:00:14.702 --> 00:00:15.987
NOT IN is not always wrong.

00:00:15.987 --> 00:00:22.367
The important point is that you must understand the possible NULL values before relying on it.

00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:27.657
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:27.657 --> 00:00:31.087
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:31.087 --> 00:00:35.547
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:35.547 --> 00:00:38.542
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:00:38.542 --> 00:00:45.492
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:00:45.492 --> 00:00:52.382
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project: validate the meaning of the result,

00:00:52.382 --> 00:00:54.339
not only whether the statement executes.
